# Why PayPal Forced Me to Think Differently About Online Income.

**Published:** 2026-06-07
**Link:** https://hakunashortcut.substack.com/p/why-paypal-forced-me-to-think-differently

---

The first time I saw a payment notification from an online client, I felt like I had won the lottery. A few taps later, I was already building castles in the air, doing mental calculations.

*"$$$$s to Kenya shillings."*

The conversions were happening instantly — and that money already had a budget.

- Part of it would buy enough electricity tokens (no more interruptions).
- Part of it would buy a data package plan to cover internet (no more daily bundles).
- Maybe a small reward for bagging my first American dollar, surviving the digital jungle while chasing opportunities online.

Sitting at a wooden table in rural Kenya, the internet suddenly felt real. For a brief moment, it felt like the distance between Nyandarua and Silicon Valley had disappeared.

Then reality arrived. Not through a client, not through a missed deadline, and not through poor work.

It arrived through a notification:

**Your account has been limited.**

Every freelancer knows that feeling. You break a sweat. Your stomach drops. Your mind starts racing. You check your balance twice. You refresh the page. You search Google.

*No!* You convince yourself it must be a mistake.

Then the requests begin: *Proof of address. Utility bills. Documentation. Verification.*

The problem was simple:

The system expected me to live in a world that looked very different from mine. It expected a world of street names, apartment numbers, water bills, and neatly documented addresses.

In Nyandarua, the reality hits different. My electricity comes through M-Pesa. Water comes from local sources. Directions are given using landmarks, not street numbers. How do you explain that to an automated system designed thousands of kilometers away?

For days, I focused on solving the PayPal problem, eventually realizing I was asking the wrong questions.

The real question wasn't: "How do I unlock my account?" The real question was: "Why is a single account capable of stopping my entire income stream?"

That realization changed everything. I stopped thinking only about earning online; I started thinking about infrastructure. Payment rails. Backup systems. Alternative withdrawal methods. Multiple ways to receive money.

Because the internet rewards flexibility.

> **If one platform can freeze your business, then you don't own a business.**
>
> **You own an account.**
>
> **And those are two very different things.**

PayPal didn't teach me how to make money online; it taught me something more valuable. It taught me to build systems that survive when things go wrong. Because in the digital jungle, every watering hole eventually dries up.

The people who survive are the ones who know where the next one is.

